Now, $y = x^2$ gives $\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2x.$ So the tangent at $x = 1$ has slope $m = 2.$ The line with slope $m = -\dfrac{1}{2}$ perpendicular to it is $y = -\dfrac{1}{2}x + b.$ Plugging in the point $A = (1,1),$ we get $1 = -\dfrac{1}{2} + b.$ Therefore, $b = \dfrac{3}{2},$ and so the normal line is $y = -\dfrac{1}{2}x + \dfrac{3}{2}.$</think>

